  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Golovinomyces bolayi is a fungal pathogen belonging to the powdery mildew family, characterized by white, powdery fungal growth on plant surfaces. This obligate parasite affects various host plants primarily in temperate regions including New England.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "White Powdery Coating",
      "description": "Distinctive white to grayish powder covering leaves, stems, and flowers, which can be easily wiped off",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Microscopic Spores",
      "description": "Under magnification, conidiophores (spore-bearing structures) appear as foot-shaped or erect chains of spores",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Leaf Symptoms",
      "description": "Infected leaves show yellowing and eventual necrosis as the fungus progresses, often on upper leaf surfaces first",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Golovinomyces bolayi thrives in cool to moderate temperatures with high humidity levels typical of New England's spring and fall conditions, commonly found on susceptible host plants in gardens and wild vegetation.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Powdery mildews like Golovinomyces bolayi are unique among fungi because they can reproduce and spread without requiring a wet surface, making them prolific even in drier conditions compared to other fungal pathogens.",
